Scientific attribution
----------------------
This project is an independent reimplementation inspired by:

  Christopher J. Shallue and Andrew Vanderburg (2018),
  "Identifying Exoplanets with Deep Learning: A Five-planet Resonant Chain
  around Kepler-80 and an Eighth Planet around Kepler-90",
  The Astronomical Journal 155, 94. arXiv:1712.05044.

  AstroNet, in google-research/exoplanet-ml
  (https://github.com/google-research/exoplanet-ml), Copyright 2018 The
  Exoplanet ML Authors, licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.

No AstroNet source code is copied into this repository. Design choices
adopted from that work (global/local view lengths of 2001/201 bins, local
window of +-4 transit durations with bin width 0.16 durations, view
normalisation to median 0 and minimum -1, DR24 av_training_set label mapping
PC -> 1, AFP/NTP -> 0, UNK excluded) are reimplemented independently and are
documented in README.md.

Data
----
* Kepler Q1-Q17 DR24 Threshold Crossing Event table and Planetary Systems
  table: NASA Exoplanet Archive, operated by the California Institute of
  Technology under contract with NASA. This research has made use of the NASA
  Exoplanet Archive.
* Kepler long-cadence light curves (Data Release 25): Mikulski Archive for
  Space Telescopes (MAST) at the Space Telescope Science Institute. This
  paper includes data collected by the Kepler mission. Funding for the Kepler
  mission is provided by the NASA Science Mission Directorate.

The bundled light curves in app/examples/ are unmodified TIME, PDCSAP_FLUX,
SAP_QUALITY and QUARTER values extracted from those public MAST files.

Software
--------
Lightkurve (MIT licence) is used for the reference Kepler quality bitmask
value, which is hard-coded in src/exofinder/lightcurve.py and verified against
the installed Lightkurve in tests.
